<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramesses XI</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1070 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramesses XI was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a> and of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. During his reign real power passed to the high priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and to military commanders. His death around 1070 BCE ended the New Kingdom through administrative dissolution rather than conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi.jpg" alt="Ramesses XI" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Richard Lepsius (1810-84) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Khonsu_Ramesses_XI_Lepsius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Menmaatre Ramesses XI is generally counted the tenth and final <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>. He was once thought to be the son of Ramesses X and Queen Tyti, but research on the Harris papyrus showed that Tyti was a queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>, so his mother is unknown. He is believed to have married Tentamun, daughter of Nebseny, and to have fathered Duathathor-Henuttawy, later wife of the high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i">Pinedjem I</a>, though these connections remain tentative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He ruled for at least 29 years, and some Egyptologists think a little over 30. His highest certain date is Year 11 of the wḥm-mswt, the era called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, which equals Year 29 of his reign. The era began in his Year 19, probably to stress a return to normal conditions after a coup. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panehesy">Pinehesy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a>, had ousted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a>, Amenhotep, and for some time controlled the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><p>How this period of suppression ended, and who forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panehesy">Pinehesy</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, is unknown. He seems to have kept a power base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, and in Year 10 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> the general and High Priest Piankh went on an expedition to Nubia, though the sources do not make clear whether it was a campaign against him.</p><p>His reign has left many papyri, among them the Adoption Papyrus, accounts of tomb-robbery trials and the Late Ramesside Letters written by scribes of the Necropolis and by Piankh. In one of these letters Piankh speaks disparagingly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and orders secret arrests, which suggests that the political situation was complex and that Piankh may have acted on a hidden agenda. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-medina">Deir el-Medina</a>, the village of the royal tomb workmen, was abandoned either in his reign or shortly afterwards, apparently because the Royal Necropolis was shifted north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ramesses XI died under unknown circumstances. He prepared a tomb in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> (KV4) but left it unfinished, and he seems to have arranged to be buried away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, possibly near Memphis or at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>. In the 21st Dynasty, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinedjem-i">Pinedjem I</a>, the tomb served as a workshop for processing materials from the burials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> and perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a>.</p><p>Most Egyptologists hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smendes">Smendes</a> succeeded him as the first Lower Egyptian king of the Twenty-first Dynasty, though the problem of a possible king between them remains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herihor">Herihor</a> and Smendes founded that dynasty with a new royal capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>, while Middle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> passed in effect to the High Priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramesses XI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The death of <b>Ramesses XI</b> around 1070 BCE ended the New Kingdom not through conquest but through administrative dissolution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Places in the royal crew were prized and usually passed from father to son; the village was abandoned under <b>Ramesses XI</b>, when tomb robbery, Libyan raids and civil war brought the work to an end.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom disintegrated under <b>Ramesses XI</b>, the general and High Priest of Amun Herihor took control of Thebes around 1080 BCE and even wrote his name in a royal cartouche.</blockquote><a href="/state/theocracy-of-amun-at-thebes">Theocracy of Amun at Thebes</a></li><li><blockquote>On the death of <b>Ramesses XI</b> around 1077 BCE, Smendes, who had governed the north from the Delta, became king and founded the Twenty-first Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.98,31.88&amp;year=-1080" aria-label="Tanis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5375 1034 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ramesses XI"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5825 1304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5825 1304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.98,31.88&amp;year=-1080">What was here in 1080 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
